Good afternoon. We are one May I-GEM. We are aware that you have such a full schedule, so we thought we'd begin with a brief overview of our project, summarize our main deliverables, and present some exciting updates on what we've been up to since WikiFreeze.

2
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:25,550
Our team is from Williamsburg, Virginia, where Williamsburg and surrounding regions face really serious water challenges, including algal blooms in nearby lakes, corrosion on local ships and bridges, and pathogenic bacteria contamination in nearby cities.

3
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:28,300
We wanted to use Senbiah to address these problems.

4
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:34,580
But we found that there was a large gap in foundational knowledge of how engineered chassis actually function in the real world.

5
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:37,490
We took on a really ambitious project.

6
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:42,480
especially for a team of six people given that we started in February and no one university has worked on this before.

7
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:54,190
Rather than solely creating engineering solutions, we wanted to address the gap in foundational knowledge with the overall goal of generating design principles for employing sin bio and aquatic systems.

8
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:57,300
We did just that with the following accomplishments.

9
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:05,870
First, we created novel software tools, a query and a choir, which utilize machine learning to predict whether chassis will survive in an aquatic environment.

10
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:13,070
A query, or database with over 2,000 samples, relates metagenomic data stored in taxonomic matrices with environmental factors.

11
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:20,300
It features exclusively shotgun metagenomic data, not 16S, in order to achieve species-level taxonomic processing.

12
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:28,050
Acquire our ML software predicts chassis survival under provided conditions, considering environmental factors and community structure.

13
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:35,180
Since Wikifreeze, we've expanded a choir's coverage to over 20,000 chassis, and we're incorporating meta-transcriptomic data.

14
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:39,600
we also developed a generalizable math modeling framework.

15
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:50,160
not just for our individual case studies, but to support a broad range of aquatic applications by incorporating complex fluid dynamics and accounting for biotic and abiotic factors to acquire.

16
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:55,950
After WikiFreeze, we expanded our modeling framework to include static water conditions and created a guidebook.

17
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:05,900
We also performed controlled, environmentally relevant case studies to generate design principles and developed as a byproduct potential engineering solutions in novel parts.

18
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:12,460
We began by engineering an inducible basil-sapil system that strengthens biofoam formation for corrosion prevention.

19
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:19,380
We also optimized the B-subtiles integration vector and made it compatible with iGenPART standards for everyone to use.

20
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:31,660
We then tested engineered strains on steel and seawater microcosms and identified gene expression differences through controlled paired RNA analysis. Something we found was rare in publicly available data sets.

21
00:02:32,710 --> 00:02:37,460
Post-Wiki freeze, we studied our strains' corrosion inhibition efficiency for an extended period of time.

22
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:53,360
We then assessed two algal bloom remediation strategies. However, after conducting 250 assays searching for novel microcystis phage and from input from IHP, we found that phage did not offer feasible treatment strategies perhaps.

23
00:02:53,930 --> 00:03:11,180
We therefore assessed A. balei's bloomer mediation potential through well-controlled microcosm experiments using metagenomic and metatranscriptomic analyses. Since wikiFREES, we've continued to analyze RNA-seq data and identified over 250 DE genes and associated pathways that affect chassis behavior in realistic environments.

24
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:18,060
We also developed a phage cocktail to maximize mycobacterial lysis and prevent biofilm maturation in household pipes.

25
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:22,930
We tested it both under lab conditions and a microcosm simulating PVC pipes.

26
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:30,670
Using different forms of measurement and RNA-seq data, we generated design principles for FAGE treatments better suited for dynamic environments.

27
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:37,170
Post-Wiki-Freeze, we analyzed RNA-seq data further in order to identify almost 500 differential expressed genes.

28
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:47,920
our CART project incorporated measurements across multiple stages. This approach not only provided a comprehensive evaluation of experimental outcomes, but also promoted accessibility.

29
00:03:48,930 --> 00:04:01,200
Perhaps most importantly, we include a type of measurement not typical for iGEM. We mined the wealth of publicly available RNA-seq data and performed a meta-analysis of genes differentially expressed between lab in more dynamic conditions.

30
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:06,960
We identified paired studies in literature and performed pathway analysis for each study ourselves.

31
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:15,540
Given the substantial amount of data, thousands of samples, we then turn to using AI tools to complement this analysis, which we verified using the previous approach.

32
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:29,200
Through a meta-analysis of existing data sets, mathematical modeling, and wet lab experimentation in aquatic environments, our project generated foundational advances into how synthetic constructs actually behave in aquatic environments.

33
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:38,350
From there, we generated design principles that offer guidelines for how to select, enhance, and engineer constructs that would function more reliably in the real world.

34
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:45,420
For resources on reliable implementation, we offer a guidebook and a quick start guide for synthetic biologists and policy makers.

35
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:58,770
Circling back to where we started, we hope that these foundational insights will help enhance safety understanding and lead to more effective policy, as suggested by our RHP, to one day channel sent bio from the lab into aquatic environments.

